  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "f1",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.references.term",
        "lint.references.marked"
      ],
      "text": "lint.references.marked now matches a name of several words across any run of spaces and tabs, but lint.references.term does not say whether a term mark such as [spec  tree] or [line<tab>ending] names the entry spec tree / line ending. So an unmarked use may require a mark that then fails to resolve.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in c09c980"
    },
    {
      "id": "f2",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.references.term",
        "lint.references.term-ambiguous"
      ],
      "text": "lint.references.term now compares names ignoring case and treats any run of spaces and tabs as one space, but lint.references.term-ambiguous does not say how it compares names. Entries named \"Spec Tree\" and \"spec tree\", or \"spec tree\" and \"spec  tree\" (two spaces), in one glossary or across levels would both match one mark, and no rule says they are ambiguous.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in e439b08"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.references.term"
      ],
      "text": "A mark can stand in a \"Represents\" field, and a field value can wrap onto the next line, so a mark's words can be split by a line break. The new body joins words only across spaces and tabs, so no rule says whether such a mark resolves. Nor does any rule say how spaces or tabs at the start or end of a mark, as in \"[ spec tree]\", are treated.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in e439b08"
    },
    {
      "id": "f4",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.references.term",
        "lint.references.term-ambiguous",
        "lint.references.marked"
      ],
      "text": "lint.references.term (and through it lint.references.term-ambiguous) joins words across 'any run of white space', while lint.references.marked joins them only across 'spaces and tabs', and the Examples rows of lint.references.term also speak only of spaces and tabs. No rule says which other characters count as white space, such as a non-breaking space or other Unicode spaces, so a mark could resolve to a name that the same text, left unmarked, would not match.",
      "status": "resolved",
      "reason": "edited in d54feef"
    },
    {
      "id": "f5",
      "kind": "gap",
      "elements": [
        "lint.references.term",
        "system.dependencies"
      ],
      "text": "The new comparison sentence of lint.references.term spells out case and white space, but not Unicode form: no rule says whether a mark and a name that differ only in composed versus decomposed form (such as ü stored as u followed by a mark) are the same name. system.dependencies takes in golang.org/x/text/unicode/norm 'for reading names in the same Unicode form on every platform', and lint.layout settles NFC for file names only. lint.references.marked, lint.references.foreign and lint.references.term-ambiguous all compare names the way this rule does, so the gap reaches them too.",
      "status": "dismissed",
      "reason": "Older than this amendment; how names compare (Unicode form, case folding) becomes one statement of its own."
    }
  ]
}
